  "edits": [
    {
      "id": "facilitator-network-scope",
      "before": "We request **USD 38,000 for 400 prospective delivery hours over 150 days from actual award acceptance**. The funded outcome is **two independently operated merchants, two independent buyer operators, and the same useful service operating through two independently operated facilitators**. Delivery progresses from installed customer services to recurring testnet use, mainnet use and an independent maintainer’s recovery and handover.",
      "after": "We request **USD 38,000 for 400 prospective delivery hours over 150 days from actual award acceptance**. The funded outcome is **two independently operated merchants, two independent buyer operators, and the same useful service operating through two independently operated facilitators on testnet in T2**. T3 brings both merchants to mainnet under their reviewed production configurations, followed by an independent maintainer’s recovery and handover.",
      "reason": "Make the existing T2 testnet and T3 mainnet requirements explicit; preserve the six-package scope without adding a two-mainnet-facilitator commitment.",
      "sources": [
        "current-six-package-table",
        "stellar-x402-20261012"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "veridex-specific-invitation",
      "before": "|Veridex|Invited an open testnet design partnership after declining a third-party testing budget.|One agreed testnet route and buyer adaptation; proposed $1,500 over five business days.|",
      "after": "|Veridex|Invited our existing SF88 buyer CLI into its testnet cohort to identify external operator UX and integration findings. Its current testing milestone has no allocated paid third-party budget.|One agreed testnet exact-payment route, bounded buyer adaptation and onboarding handover; proposed $1,500 over five business days.|",
      "reason": "Replace a broad interest label with the actual external-buyer task in Emmanuel’s original response; preserve the latest controlling commercial offer.",
      "sources": [
        "veridex-human-20261011",
        "veridex-controlling-offer-20261011"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "veridex-operating-need",
      "before": "These are proposed workshares and observed conversations. The commissioning agreement and funded customer cohort remain to be selected.",
      "after": "Veridex’s invitation gives the buyer work a specific purpose. Its existing conformance suite already exercises exact payments and settlement; the requested contribution is experience with an external buyer’s onboarding and operator flow. Our proposed work carries one agreed route from the original 402 challenge through operator approval, delivered response and settlement record, then leaves reproducible configuration and a runbook. The endpoint, account, actual asset and acceptance criteria have not yet been returned. Bryce’s latest offer requires agreed scope, payment and access before the five-day delivery, or an explicit reciprocal agreement with commensurate usable benefits and rights.\n\nThese are proposed workshares and observed conversations. The commissioning agreement and funded customer cohort remain to be selected.",
      "reason": "Put a substantive customer workflow finding in the nine-answer narrative instead of relying on a linked interest table. Attribute the need to the actual invitation and keep the route and agreement at their observed stage.",
      "sources": [
        "veridex-human-20261011",
        "veridex-controlling-offer-20261011"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "medstar-concrete-workflow",
      "before": "The existing commercial model supports this adoption path: fixed-fee implementation of an agreed customer operation, followed by separately scoped care. A customer commissions the source and application adaptation; the merchant retains its resource receipts; TokenJunkieLabs invoices its agreed engineering and handover. Actual acceptance, implementation effort, corrections, support demand and collections inform following offers.",
      "after": "The existing commercial model supports this adoption path: fixed-fee implementation of an agreed customer operation, followed by separately scoped care. A customer commissions the source and application adaptation; the merchant retains its resource receipts; TokenJunkieLabs invoices its agreed engineering and handover. Actual acceptance, implementation effort, corrections, support demand and collections inform following offers.\n\n**Study-operations delivery example.** MedStar’s [published Sovereign Execution project](https://healthit.gov/interoperability/investments/leap/2026-leap-in-health-it-projects) needs taxonomy-based human review and comparison with baseline workflows. Its investigator identified HealthLab as the technical partner and forwarded our proposal. Our offered software work receives agreed Task, Consent and trace exports, carries exact case/action versions through review and adjudication, and delivers the paired tables and editable report with its original sources. The [implemented workspace and integration handover](https://github.com/woahwhattheheck/commons/blob/5f6b4b58155234262a18aa562b3058848a2dafee/opportunities/medstar_shadow_study_2026/workspace/INTEGRATION_HANDOVER.md) make that receiving task concrete. The original $12,000 / three $4,000 milestones / twenty-business-day workshare remains proposed; no supplied study data, accepted scope, payment or Stellar-use agreement is recorded. This is evidence of a defined adjacent workflow and implementation capability, separate from the selected Stellar merchant/buyer cohort.",
      "reason": "Show a source-backed complete operating task and actual human referral while separating proposed study integration from a commissioned Stellar installation.",
      "sources": [
        "medstar-public-project",
        "medstar-human-20261012",
        "medstar-controlling-followup-20261012",
        "medstar-integration-handover"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "explicit-x402-and-network-support",
      "before": "We compose existing ecosystem infrastructure. The x402 Exact Stellar scheme and facilitator supply payment verification and settlement. Vellar’s optional discovery extension publishes eligible settled resources to its catalogue. OpenZeppelin, Veridex and Periplo supply relevant payment infrastructure, while Rumble Fish is developing facilitator, discovery and SDK capabilities. The existing MPP Charge workbench retains its own challenge, authorization and recovery model. The implementation uses existing SDKs and token contracts.",
      "after": "We compose existing ecosystem infrastructure. The x402 Exact Stellar scheme and facilitator supply payment verification and settlement. Vellar’s optional discovery extension publishes eligible settled resources to its catalogue. OpenZeppelin, Veridex and Periplo supply relevant payment infrastructure, while Rumble Fish is developing facilitator, discovery and SDK capabilities. The existing MPP Charge workbench retains its own challenge, authorization and recovery model. The implementation uses existing SDKs and token contracts.\n\nThe [current Stellar x402 documentation](https://developers.stellar.org/docs/build/agentic-payments/x402) describes buyer-signed Soroban authorization entries and facilitator verification and settlement. The installation binds the actual route’s supported scheme, network, asset and payment requirements. The hosted Vellar endpoint is currently documented as testnet-only. T2’s same-service comparison uses two actually compatible testnet configurations; T3 uses each merchant’s reviewed mainnet-capable configuration. A discovery listing or different offer catalogue alone does not establish same-service substitution.",
      "reason": "Explain the specific Stellar authorization role and current facilitator-network boundary from the original documentation, without assigning an unconfirmed provider to a funded customer.",
      "sources": [
        "stellar-x402-20261012"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "exact-query-truncation",
      "before": "|Paid agent query|The MariaDB HPD Preservation/minimum-20 query returned all 100 rows,14 columns and 53,468 bytes for one 50,000-stroop purchase. Scalar strings and the declared truncation indicator were retained. The purchase reopened with signing disconnected, and the complete result reopened in another directory.|",
      "after": "|Paid agent query|The MariaDB HPD Preservation/minimum-20 query delivered its complete returned result: 100 rows, 14 columns and 53,468 bytes for one 50,000-stroop purchase. Its original truncation flag is **true**; this is the complete response, not a claim to the whole database population. Scalar strings were retained. The purchase reopened with signing disconnected, and the complete result reopened in another directory.|",
      "reason": "Expose the actual source truncation value so complete-byte receiving is not mistaken for complete-database coverage.",
      "sources": [
        "original-hpd-paid-query"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "query-financial-source-binding",
      "before": "These operations demonstrate the common path from exact source/query selection through payment to a complete result that another operator can use.",
      "after": "The paid query completed on October 11 at 20:12:58 UTC. Its full result SHA256 is `4c22f8a26d48156c3e07543328b62ca6f993ce60856166d6805dc0660d9acb8d`; native transaction `50d61d1102bfcf960af44171e5248e0899ed6d99cec1951a62075f9a65a6cbed` is retained at ledger 5,145,195. These identifiers connect the demonstrated output and the original transfer without counting a reopened file as another purchase.\n\nThese operations demonstrate the common path from exact source/query selection through payment to a complete result that another operator can use.",
      "reason": "Give the reviewer a concrete output/transfer binding while retaining the completed original operation and count.",
      "sources": [
        "original-hpd-paid-query"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "modeled-resource-status",
      "before": "The awarded schedule will use the actual acceptance date, named contributors and their working availability.",
      "after": "The awarded schedule will use the actual acceptance date, named contributors and their working availability. The 25 + 8 hours/week allocation is a scheduling scenario, not a secured staffing commitment; named allocations remain to be confirmed. The $0 incremental compute reserve likewise does not establish customer host capacity or a fee-payer commitment.",
      "reason": "Clarify the current resource evidence without changing Bryce’s modeled allocation, budget, delivery dates or cash assumptions.",
